# When does cooperation emerge anyway?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "What changes when the game is repeated? What did Axelrod's tournaments show about tit for tat?"
next: {"title":"Price war or quiet collusion?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/40-price-war-or-quiet-collusion.cast"}

## Live and let live
sources:
    - id: ashworth
      title: 'Trench Warfare 1914–1918: The Live and Let Live System'
      authors: Tony Ashworth
      year: 1980
      finding: 'From soldiers'' diaries and letters: in many quiet sectors of the Western Front, opposing units tacitly held fire and ritualised their shelling, so that both sides could avoid casualties.'
    - id: axelrod84
      title: The Evolution of Cooperation (ch. 4, The live-and-let-live system in trench warfare in World War I)
      authors: Robert Axelrod
      year: 1984
      finding: 'Reads Ashworth''s trench truces as an iterated prisoner''s dilemma: the same small units faced each other for long periods, so restraint could be repaid and shooting punished.'

    path g_out closed #3d3833 points [[270,179],[730,179],[730,479],[270,479]] hidden true
    path g_v #3d3833 points [[500,179],[500,479]] hidden true
    path g_h #3d3833 points [[270,329],[730,329]] hidden true
    polygon nash_1_1 #f2c14e points [[502,181],[728,181],[728,327],[502,327]] style.fill #f2c14e style.opacity 0.45 hidden true
    text row_label_0 "Hold fire" #8f887c x 200 y 404 font_size 30 hidden true
    text row_label_1 "Shoot" #8f887c x 200 y 254 font_size 30 hidden true
    text col_label_0 "Hold fire" #8f887c x 385 y 510 font_size 30 hidden true
    text col_label_1 "Shoot" #8f887c x 615 y 510 font_size 30 hidden true
    text col_player_label "German unit" x 500 y 560 font_size 30 hidden true
    text row_player_label "British unit" x 150 y 510 font_size 30 hidden true
    text cell_0_0 "3, 3" x 385 y 404 font_size 44 hidden true
    text cell_0_1 "0, 5" x 615 y 404 font_size 44 hidden true
    text cell_1_0 "5, 0" x 385 y 254 font_size 44 hidden true
    text cell_1_1 "1, 1" x 615 y 254 font_size 44 hidden true
    group grid members ["g_out","g_v","g_h"] hidden true
    text front "Western Front, 1915" x 500 y 520 font_size 40 hidden true
    icon helm1 size 110 set twemoji x 260 y 380 or ["helmet","soldier"] icon_look picture of "military helmet" hidden true
    icon helm2 size 110 set twemoji x 740 y 380 or ["helmet","soldier"] flip true icon_look picture of "military helmet" hidden true
    text quiet "holding fire" #2f6b8f cites ["ashworth"] x 500 y 380 font_size 34 hidden true
    text hour "shelling at fixed hours" #8f887c cites ["ashworth"] x 500 y 250 font_size 30 hidden true
    text again "same enemy,\nday after day" #2f6b8f cites ["axelrod84"] x 868 y 420 font_size 30 hidden true
    text end100 "War ends:\nday 100" #b5482e x 868 y 420 font_size 34 hidden true
    text u1 "Day 100 → shoot" #b5482e x 872 y 460 font_size 28 hidden true
    text u2 "Day 99 → shoot" #b5482e x 872 y 410 font_size 28 hidden true
    text u3 "… Day 1 → shoot" #b5482e x 872 y 360 font_size 28 hidden true

    card title "Live and let live"

When do rivals cooperate, even though each would gain by cheating? Start where coope (@draw helm1 helm2 parallel true@)ration had no business existing: the trenches of the First World War.
    draw front

B: Facing units could shoot at each other  (@draw quiet@)every day. Yet along quiet stretches of the line, both sides held fire, and some shelled at the same hour eac (@draw hour@)h day, so the other side knew where not to stand.

    erase front helm1 helm2 quiet hour

Put it in a table, with illustrative points.
    draw grid row_player_label col_player_label row_label_0 row_label_1 col_label_0 col_label_1

Both hold fire: three each.
    draw cell_0_0

B: Shoot while they hold fire, and you get five, they get nothing. Both shoot: one e (@draw cell_1_1 ends@)ach.
    draw cell_1_0 cell_0_1

Whatever the other side does, shooting pays more.
    highlight cell_1_0 cell_1_1 effect box

That's the prisoner's dile (@draw nash_1_1@)mma: both shoot, and both end up with one instead of three.

B: So the table says shoot. The soldier (@highlight cell_0_0 effect circle@)s didn't. What did they know that the table leaves out?

That they would mee (@draw again@)t again tomorrow. The same units often faced each other for weeks or months.

    erase again

B: Then try this. Say both sides know th (@draw end100@)e war ends in exactly a hundred days.

    quiz "Both sides know the war ends on day 100. Do they hold fire on day 1?" judge false voice b pool false
        right: Here is how strict logic answers.
        * Yes, a hundred days is a long time
        * No, it unravels from the end

    erase end100

Strict logic says no. On d (@draw u1@)ay one hundred there is no tomorrow to prote (@highlight nash_1_1 effect pulse@)ct, so both shoot.

B: Which makes day ninety-nine the real last day. So they shoot then too, and the truce unravel (@draw u3@)s all the way back to day one.
    draw u2

So a known end kills cooperation. What keeps it alive is not knowing when the game will end.
    highlight nash_1_1 effect pulse

    erase u1 u2 u3

## The shadow of the future
domain: {"x":[0,0.85],"y":[0,18]}
vars:
    d:
      value: 0
      min: 0
      max: 0.8
    h:
      expr: 3/(1-d)
    sv:
      expr: 5 + d/(1-d)
sources:
    - id: axelrod84
      title: The Evolution of Cooperation
      authors: Robert Axelrod
      year: 1984
      finding: 'Coins ''the shadow of the future'': cooperation based on reciprocity is stable only if the next meeting matters enough relative to the present.'

    axes ax x_label "δ: chance of another round" y_label "total points" ticks.x [0,0.25,0.5] ticks.y [0,5,10,15] hidden true
    curve c0 #2f6b8f expr 3/(1-x) x_from 0 x_to 0.82 hidden true
    curve c1 #b5482e expr "5 + x/(1-x)" x_from 0 x_to 0.85 hidden true
    curve c2 dashed #8f887c expr 5/(1-x) x_from 0 x_to 0.72 hidden true
    label lab_hold "hold fire" left #2f6b8f attach_to c0 hidden true
    label lab_shoot "shoot" right #b5482e attach_to c1 hidden true
    label lab_sucker "partner never hits back" left #8f887c attach_to c2 hidden true
    dot pc #2f6b8f at.on c0 at.x 0 bind {"at.x":"d"} hidden true
    dot pd #b5482e at.on c1 at.x 0 bind {"at.x":"d"} hidden true
    text ro "δ = {d:2}   hold fire {h:1}   shoot {sv:1}" x 440 y 610 font_size 34 hidden true
    dot cross at.on c0 at.x 0.5 hidden true
    label lab_cross "δ = ½: both 6" above-left attach_to cross hidden true
    text shadow "the shadow of the future" at.data [0.2,14] cites ["axelrod84"] font_size 34 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"test"}] then test hidden true
    scratch sc work [{"tex":"3+3\\delta+3\\delta^2+\\dots = 3/(1-\\delta)"},{"tex":"5+\\delta+\\delta^2+\\dots = 5+\\delta/(1-\\delta)"},{"tex":"3 = 5(1-\\delta)+\\delta \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\delta = 1/2"},{"tex":"\\delta \\ge \\frac{T-R}{T-P} = \\frac{5-3}{5-1}"}] x 360 y 530 font_size 30 hidden true

    card title "The shadow of the future"

B: Unknown endings let us count differently. Call delta the chance th (@highlight ax effect underline@)at, after this round, there will be another.
    draw ax

B: Hold fire with a partner who does the same: three points now, three more if there's a next round, and so on.
    draw c0 lab_hold

B: Or shoot: five points today. But your partner strikes back, and from then on it's one point a round.
    draw c1 lab_shoot

With no tomorrow, delta zero (@draw pc pd ro@), shooting wins: five against three. That's the one-shot dilemma.

B: Now raise the odds of meeting again. The stream of threes grows much faster than the stream of ones.
    animate d 0.8 duration 5

They cross at  (@draw cross lab_cross@)one half. If the odds of another round are better than even, holding fire pays.

    erase pc pd ro

B: Robert Axelrod called this the shadow of the  (@draw shadow@)future. The longer it falls across today, the more cooperation pays.

    erase shadow

And it only works against a partner who strikes back (@draw c2 lab_sucker@). Against one who always holds fire, shooting pays at any delta.

    erase c2 lab_sucker

B: So repetition doesn't guarantee cooperation. It makes it one equilibrium among many, alongside endless shooting.
    highlight c0 c1

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own voice b
        intro: Want to see where one half comes from?

@math

    erase mathq

Three every round, discounted by delta each time, sums to three over one minus delta.
    draw sc_box sc_line_1

Five once, then one a round, sums to five plus delta over one minus delta.
    draw sc_line_2

Set them equal and delta comes out at one half.
    draw sc_line_3

In general, the temptation gain over the punishment loss.
    draw sc_line_4

    erase sc

@test

    erase mathq

## Axelrod's tournament
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - Always C
      - Always D
      - Tit for tat
      - Grudger
      - Prober
    values: '{sim.avg}'
    value_labels: true
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 5
    y_label: points per round
    box:
      x: 320
      'y': 130
      w: 630
      h: 520
sources:
    - id: axelrod80a
      title: Effective Choice in the Prisoner's Dilemma
      authors: Robert Axelrod
      year: 1980
      finding: 'First computer tournament: 14 entries (plus a random rule), each played every other for 200 rounds; Anatol Rapoport''s tit for tat, the shortest program, scored best.'
    - id: axelrod80b
      title: More Effective Choice in the Prisoner's Dilemma
      authors: Robert Axelrod
      year: 1980
      finding: 'Second tournament: 62 entries from contestants who had seen the first results; tit for tat won again.'
    - id: axelrod84
      title: The Evolution of Cooperation
      authors: Robert Axelrod
      year: 1984
      finding: 'Tit for tat''s success: nice (never defects first), retaliatory, forgiving and clear; it never scores more than its partner in a game.'

    text which "Which rule wins?" x 500 y 560 font_size 44 hidden true
    text axname "Robert Axelrod" #8a5fa8 x 500 y 560 font_size 36 hidden true
    text t1980 "1980: 14 programs\nevery pair, 200 rounds" cites ["axelrod80a"] x 500 y 400 font_size 36 hidden true
    portrait portrait_rapoport x 860 y 430 width 200 of "Anatol Rapoport" cameo true hidden true
    text tft "Tit for tat:\ncooperate first,\nthen copy" #2f6b8f x 400 y 400 font_size 40 hidden true
    text t2 "Round two: 62 entries.\nTit for tat again." cites ["axelrod80b"] x 400 y 230 font_size 34 hidden true
    ```brython sim x 150 y 470 width 260 show code controls ["rounds"] pane controls hidden true
    rounds = Slider(1, 200, default=1, label="Rounds per game")
    P = {"CC": 3, "CD": 0, "DC": 5, "DD": 1}
    names = ["AllC", "AllD", "TFT", "Grudger", "Prober"]
    def move(s, me, you):
        if s == "AllC": return "C"
        if s == "AllD": return "D"
        if s == "Grudger": return "D" if "D" in you else "C"
        if s == "Prober" and len(me) == 1: return "D"
        return you[-1] if you else "C"
    total = {n: 0 for n in names}
    for i, a in enumerate(names):
        for b in names[i:]:
            ma, mb = [], []
            for r in range(rounds):
                x, y = move(a, ma, mb), move(b, mb, ma)
                ma.append(x); mb.append(y)
                total[a] += P[x + y]
                if a != b: total[b] += P[y + x]
    avg = [round(total[n] / (rounds * 5), 2) for n in names]
    ```
    text rules "nice\nstrikes back\nforgives\nclear" #2f6b8f cites ["axelrod84"] x 150 y 260 font_size 32 hidden true
    text rd "rounds: {sim.rounds}" x 150 y 560 font_size 40 hidden true

    card title "Axelrod's tournament"

Holding fire can pay, but only against the right partner. S (@draw which@)o which rule does best when you don't know who you'll meet?

    erase which

B: In 1980 the political scientist Robert Axelrod ran a contest to find out. Game theorists sent in computer pro (@draw t1980@)grams, and each played every other, two hundred rounds, with the same points as our table.
    draw axname

    erase axname

B: Fourteen entries. Some were long and cunning.
    highlight t1980 effect underline

    quiz "Which kind of program won Axelrod's first tournament?" judge false voice b pool false
        right: Tit for tat: the simplest one, which copies the other's last move.
        * A clever one that exploits others
        * One that always cooperates
        * The simplest: copy the other's last move

    erase t1980

The winner was the shortest program of all: tit for tat, sent in by Anatol Rapoport. Cooperate first, then do whatever the other did last round.
    draw portrait_rapoport tft

    erase portrait_rapoport

B: Axelrod ran a second tournament. S (@draw t2@)ixty-two entries, written by people who knew tit for tat had won. It won again.

    erase tft t2

Here's a small tournament you can run: five rules, every pair plays, and the slider sets how many rounds each game lasts.
    draw sim sim_ctl_rounds rd axes bar_1 bar_2 bar_3 bar_4 bar_5

B: With one round, always defect comes out on top. That's the one-shot dilemma again.
    highlight bar_2 effect box

Stretch the game, and by five rounds tit for tat leads, whi (@run code sim values.rounds [10,20,50,100,200]@)le always defect falls behind every rule that starts nice.
    run code sim values.rounds [1,2,3,5] every 0.6

    run code sim values.rounds [1]

Try it: slide the number of rounds, and find where always defect loses its lead.
    explore code sim

B: Odd thing: tit for tat never outscores (@highlight bar_3 effect box@) the player in front of it. It wins by getting others to cooperate, not by beating them.
    run code sim values.rounds [200]

Axelrod's summary of why it won: be nice, (@draw rules@) never shoot first; strike back; forgive quickly; and be clear enough that others can read you.

## When copying goes wrong
sources:
    - id: nowak93
      title: A strategy of win-stay, lose-shift that outperforms tit-for-tat in the Prisoner's Dilemma game
      authors: Martin Nowak, Karl Sigmund
      year: 1993
      finding: In simulations with occasional mistakes, win-stay lose-shift (Pavlov) corrects errors and outperforms tit for tat; generous tit for tat (Nowak & Sigmund 1992) also does well under noise.
      doi: 10.1038/364056a0
    - id: dalbo18
      title: 'On the Determinants of Cooperation in Infinitely Repeated Games: A Survey'
      authors: Pedro Dal Bó, Guillaume R. Fréchette
      year: 2018
      finding: 'Meta-data from many lab experiments: cooperation is more likely when it can be supported in equilibrium and rises with the continuation probability, but equilibrium alone does not ensure high cooperation; that needs cooperation to be robust to strategic uncertainty.'
      doi: 10.1257/jel.20160980
    - id: selten86
      title: 'End behavior in sequences of finite Prisoner''s Dilemma supergames: A learning theory approach'
      authors: Reinhard Selten, Rolf Stoecker
      year: 1986
      finding: Experienced subjects in ten-round prisoner's dilemmas cooperated tacitly until shortly before the end, then defected (an end effect), contrary to full backward-induction unravelling.

    text labA "A: tit for tat" x 150 y 480 font_size 34 hidden true
    text labB "B: tit for tat" x 150 y 380 font_size 34 hidden true
    text key "blue: cooperate   red: defect" #8f887c x 600 y 610 font_size 32 hidden true
    ellipse a1 #2f6b8f x 320 y 480 rx 24 ry 24 style.fill #2f6b8f hidden true
    ellipse a2 #2f6b8f x 384 y 480 rx 24 ry 24 style.fill #2f6b8f hidden true
    ellipse a3 #2f6b8f x 448 y 480 rx 24 ry 24 style.fill #2f6b8f hidden true
    ellipse a4 #b5482e x 512 y 480 rx 24 ry 24 style.fill #b5482e hidden true
    ellipse a5 #2f6b8f x 576 y 480 rx 24 ry 24 style.fill #2f6b8f hidden true
    ellipse a6 #b5482e x 640 y 480 rx 24 ry 24 style.fill #b5482e hidden true
    ellipse a7 #2f6b8f x 704 y 480 rx 24 ry 24 style.fill #2f6b8f hidden true
    ellipse a8 #b5482e x 768 y 480 rx 24 ry 24 style.fill #b5482e hidden true
    ellipse a9 #2f6b8f x 832 y 480 rx 24 ry 24 style.fill #2f6b8f hidden true
    ellipse a10 #b5482e x 896 y 480 rx 24 ry 24 style.fill #b5482e hidden true
    ellipse b1 #2f6b8f x 320 y 380 rx 24 ry 24 style.fill #2f6b8f hidden true
    ellipse b2 #2f6b8f x 384 y 380 rx 24 ry 24 style.fill #2f6b8f hidden true
    ellipse b3 #2f6b8f x 448 y 380 rx 24 ry 24 style.fill #2f6b8f hidden true
    ellipse b4 #2f6b8f x 512 y 380 rx 24 ry 24 style.fill #2f6b8f hidden true
    ellipse b5 #b5482e x 576 y 380 rx 24 ry 24 style.fill #b5482e hidden true
    ellipse b6 #2f6b8f x 640 y 380 rx 24 ry 24 style.fill #2f6b8f hidden true
    ellipse b7 #b5482e x 704 y 380 rx 24 ry 24 style.fill #b5482e hidden true
    ellipse b8 #2f6b8f x 768 y 380 rx 24 ry 24 style.fill #2f6b8f hidden true
    ellipse b9 #b5482e x 832 y 380 rx 24 ry 24 style.fill #b5482e hidden true
    ellipse b10 #2f6b8f x 896 y 380 rx 24 ry 24 style.fill #2f6b8f hidden true
    text oops "mistake" #b5482e x 512 y 530 font_size 30 hidden true
    text echo "the echo" #b5482e x 800 y 300 font_size 34 hidden true
    text wsls "win-stay, lose-shift\ngenerous tit for tat" #2f6b8f cites ["nowak93"] x 580 y 240 font_size 36 hidden true
    text cons "repetition makes it possible:\nCONSENSUS" #2f6b8f x 330 y 240 font_size 36 hidden true
    text lab "lab: more δ, more cooperation" cites ["dalbo18"] x 500 y 610 font_size 32 hidden true
    text sel "known end: cooperate until near it" cites ["selten86"] x 500 y 610 font_size 32 hidden true
    text cont "best rule:\nCONTESTED" #b5482e x 780 y 240 font_size 36 hidden true
    text firms "firms: same rivals, year after year" #8a5fa8 x 500 y 610 font_size 34 hidden true

    card title "When copying goes wrong"

Tit for tat wins where the game goes on. But real games are noisy.
    draw labA labB key a1 b1 a2 b2 a3 b3

B: Two tit-for-tat players, cooperating round after round. Then, in (@draw a4 b4 oops@) round four, one shot goes off by accident.

The other copies it next round. Then the f (@draw a6 b6@)irst copies that. The accident echoes (@draw b7 a7 a8 b8 a9 b9 a10 b10 echo@) back and forth, for ever.
    draw b5 a5

    erase oops

B: Martin Nowak and Karl Sigmund found in 1993 that a rule called win-sta (@draw wsls@)y, lose-shift recovers from such mistakes, and versions of tit for tat that sometimes forgive do well too.

    erase wsls echo

So how sure are we? That repetition makes coop (@draw cons@)eration possible is consensus in game theory.

    erase key

B: In the lab, cooperation ris (@draw lab@)es with the chance of meeting again. But being an equilibrium isn't enough: people cooperate a lot only when it's safe even if they doubt the other side.

    erase lab

And theory's unravelling? In Selten and Stoecker (@draw sel@)'s 1986 experiment, people cooperated until shortly before the known end.

    erase sel

B: Which rule is best is con (@draw cont@)tested. It depends on the noise and on who else is playing.

So cooperation emerges when the same players expect to meet again, nobody knows when it ends, and each repays kindness with kindness and betrayal with betrayal, but forgives soon enough.
    highlight a1 b1 a2 b2 a3 b3 effect box

    erase labA labB a1 a2 a3 a4 a5 a6 a7 a8 a9 a10 b1 b2 b3 b4 b5 b6 b7 b8 b9 b10 cons cont

    text test "Three new cases" #8f887c x 500 y 620 font_size 30

    icon qi1 size 120 set twemoji x 500 y 460 or ["fishing pole"] icon_look picture of fish

    quiz "Two Harbourtown fishers share the fishing grounds, and each could overfish. When is restraint most likely?" intro "Three new cases."
        right: Side by side for decades: a long, open-ended future makes today's restraint worth more than today's extra catch.
        wrong: Ask how long the shadow of the future falls: here, the fishers who stay side by side for decades.
        * One retires next month, and both know it
        + They'll fish side by side for decades
        * They sign a promise but never meet again

    erase qi1

    icon qi2 size 120 set twemoji x 500 y 460 or ["shopping cart"] icon_look picture of star

    quiz "Why does a seller on a site with public reviews ship honestly to a buyer she'll never see again?" voice b
        right: Reviews tie each sale to her future buyers: one sale becomes a round in a repeated game with everyone who reads them.
        wrong: Who else sees what she does? Her future buyers, through the reviews.
        + Reviews tie each sale to all her future buyers
        * Honesty always pays in a one-off deal
        * The buyer can't cheat her

    erase qi2

    icon qi3 size 120 set twemoji x 500 y 460 or ["handshake"] icon_look picture of "waving hand"

    quiz "Two neighbours play strict tit for tat. A greeting is missed by accident, and one thinks it was a snub. What follows?"
        right: An endless back-and-forth: each copies the other's last move, so one accidental snub echoes for ever unless someone forgives.
        wrong: Each copies the other's last move, so the snub echoes back and forth.
        * A quick return to friendship
        * Both stop greeting at once
        + An endless back-and-forth of snubs

    erase test

    erase qi3

B: Which is exactly why competition authorit (@draw firms cons cont@)ies watch firms that meet in the same market year after year.

