      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:1c5x0mf'
    '|b||Here is how strict logic answers.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 2680
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow'
    '|a||Strict logic says no. On day one hundred there is no tomorrow to protect, so both shoot.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6560
      voice: gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy
    '|b||Which makes day ninety-nine the real last day. So they shoot then too, and the truce unravels all the way back to day one.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7040
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:u35rtd'
    '|a||So a known end kills cooperation. What keeps it alive is not knowing when the game will end.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7040
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:mg02p1'
    '|b||Unknown endings let us count differently. Call delta the chance that, after this round, there will be another.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6960
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow'
    '|b||Hold fire with a partner who does the same: three points now, three more if there''s a next round, and so on.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6560
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:5ei72s'
    '|b||Or shoot: five points today. But your partner strikes back, and from then on it''s one point a round.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6160
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:uiotz7'
    '|a||With no tomorrow, delta zero, shooting wins: five against three. That''s the one-shot dilemma.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6600
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:n2pwmb'
    '|b||Now raise the odds of meeting again. The stream of threes grows much faster than the stream of ones.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6640
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:1273zyn'
    '|a||They cross at one half. If the odds of another round are better than even, holding fire pays.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6360
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:wm75nk'
    '|b||Robert Axelrod called this the shadow of the future. The longer it falls across today, the more cooperation pays.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7360
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:uiuhlf'
    '|a||And it only works against a partner who strikes back. Against one who always holds fire, shooting pays at any delta.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7440
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:1kvnk33'
    '|b||So repetition doesn''t guarantee cooperation. It makes it one equilibrium among many, alongside endless shooting.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8280
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:w4hg1t'
    '|b||Want to see where one half comes from? Show the math, or skip it?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 3920
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:110jntr'
    '|a||Three every round, discounted by delta each time, sums to three over one minus delta.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7320
      voice: gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy
    '|a||Five once, then one a round, sums to five plus delta over one minus delta.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6280
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:15vfyo7'
    '|a||Set them equal and delta comes out at one half.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 3880
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:3y9rqo'
    '|a||In general, the temptation gain over the punishment loss.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 4560
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:1el2dii'
    '|a||Holding fire can pay, but only against the right partner. So which rule does best when you don''t know who you''ll meet?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8560
      voice: gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy
    '|b||In 1980 the political scientist Robert Axelrod ran a contest to find out. Game theorists sent in computer programs, and each played every other, two hundred rounds, with the same points as our table.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 11880
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:o4qhi8'
    '|b||Fourteen entries. Some were long and cunning.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 3800
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:12j17xv'
    '|b||Which kind of program won Axelrod''s first tournament?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 3360
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:qu3v56'
    '|b||Tit for tat: the simplest one, which copies the other''s last move.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 3240
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow'
    '|a||The winner was the shortest program of all: tit for tat, sent in by Anatol Rapoport. Cooperate first, then do whatever the other did last round.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10000
      voice: gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy
    '|b||Axelrod ran a second tournament. Sixty-two entries, written by people who knew tit for tat had won. It won again.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7880
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:148c0l'
    '|a||Here''s a small tournament you can run: five rules, every pair plays, and the slider sets how many rounds each game lasts.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7840
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:kqs0pt'
    '|b||With one round, always defect comes out on top. That''s the one-shot dilemma again.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6040
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:12x8fwi'
    '|a||Stretch the game, and by five rounds tit for tat leads, while always defect falls behind every rule that starts nice.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8880
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:qipewf'
    '|a||Try it: slide the number of rounds, and find where always defect loses its lead.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5120
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:10mol12'
    '|b||Odd thing: tit for tat never outscores the player in front of it. It wins by getting others to cooperate, not by beating them.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7160
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow'
    '|a||Axelrod''s summary of why it won: be nice, never shoot first; strike back; forgive quickly; and be clear enough that others can read you.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9240
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:123zzas'
    '|a||Tit for tat wins where the game goes on. But real games are noisy.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6280
      voice: gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy
    '|b||Two tit-for-tat players, cooperating round after round. Then, in round four, one shot goes off by accident.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7880
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:141tgid'
    '|a||The other copies it next round. Then the first copies that. The accident echoes back and forth, for ever.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6800
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:mpydsj'
    '|b||Martin Nowak and Karl Sigmund found in 1993 that a rule called win-stay, lose-shift recovers from such mistakes, and versions of tit for tat that sometimes forgive do well too.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 12680
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:1a1xas1'
    '|a||So how sure are we? That repetition makes cooperation possible is consensus in game theory.':
